1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,618
Imagine you're born in a Maya city,

2
00:00:02,638 --> 00:00:04,823
about thirteen hundred years ago.

3
00:00:05,343 --> 00:00:07,169
Your money might be chocolate.

4
00:00:07,189 --> 00:00:09,446
Your head might be shaped by a board.

5
00:00:09,466 --> 00:00:11,601
And the sky might choose your name.

6
00:00:12,121 --> 00:00:13,652
By the end of this video,

7
00:00:13,672 --> 00:00:15,328
you'll know how you'd live,

8
00:00:15,348 --> 00:00:17,314
what you'd eat, what you'd fear,

9
00:00:17,334 --> 00:00:20,045
and why your city would one day be swallowed

10
00:00:20,065 --> 00:00:20,913
by the jungle.

11
00:00:21,384 --> 00:00:23,097
But your story doesn't end there.

12
00:00:25,318 --> 00:00:28,301
It's around the year seven hundred and fifty.

13
00:00:28,321 --> 00:00:30,905
You're born in a pole and thatch house,

14
00:00:30,925 --> 00:00:32,841
near the great city of Tikal,

15
00:00:32,861 --> 00:00:34,509
in what is now Guatemala.

16
00:00:34,979 --> 00:00:37,278
Your first days are a little strange.

17
00:00:37,298 --> 00:00:39,785
Your mother straps you to a cradleboard,

18
00:00:39,805 --> 00:00:42,668
with a flat board pressing gently on your soft

19
00:00:42,688 --> 00:00:43,044
skull.

20
00:00:43,465 --> 00:00:46,602
Over months, your head grows longer and flatter.

21
00:00:46,622 --> 00:00:48,575
To the Maya, this isn't cruel.

22
00:00:48,595 --> 00:00:50,219
It may protect your soul,

23
00:00:50,239 --> 00:00:53,245
and it shows the world which people you belong

24
00:00:53,265 --> 00:00:53,442
to.

25
00:00:53,912 --> 00:00:56,354
And your name? It can come from the day

26
00:00:56,374 --> 00:00:59,004
you were born, in a sacred calendar of two

27
00:00:59,024 --> 00:01:00,455
hundred and sixty days.

28
00:01:00,875 --> 00:01:02,571
So before you can even walk,

29
00:01:02,591 --> 00:01:04,165
you already carry the sky,

30
00:01:04,185 --> 00:01:06,004
and your people, on your head.

31
00:01:08,224 --> 00:01:10,676
Your home is simple, but crowded.

32
00:01:10,696 --> 00:01:13,748
A few small houses around a shared patio,

33
00:01:13,768 --> 00:01:16,821
raised on a stone platform to stay dry in

34
00:01:16,841 --> 00:01:18,094
the rainy season.

35
00:01:18,515 --> 00:01:22,059
Grandparents, parents, aunts, cousins. Everyone works,

36
00:01:22,079 --> 00:01:24,105
eats and sleeps close together,

37
00:01:24,125 --> 00:01:25,030
on woven mats.

38
00:01:25,450 --> 00:01:26,897
Dogs bark in the yard.

39
00:01:26,917 --> 00:01:29,097
Turkeys strut between the houses.

40
00:01:29,117 --> 00:01:32,365
Smoke from the cooking fire drifts up through the

41
00:01:32,385 --> 00:01:32,831
thatch.

42
00:01:33,302 --> 00:01:36,160
And here's something that might surprise you.

43
00:01:36,180 --> 00:01:39,614
Many Maya families buried their dead right beneath the

44
00:01:39,634 --> 00:01:43,068
floors of their homes. Your ancestors aren't far away.

45
00:01:43,088 --> 00:01:44,603
They're under your feet.

46
00:01:45,073 --> 00:01:47,233
For your family, that's not creepy.

47
00:01:47,253 --> 00:01:49,536
It's comforting. The dead stay close,

48
00:01:49,556 --> 00:01:51,965
and they still belong to the household.

49
00:01:54,185 --> 00:01:57,459
Now look around. Tikal is not a lonely ruin

50
00:01:57,479 --> 00:01:59,451
in the jungle. It's alive.

51
00:01:59,870 --> 00:02:02,432
Stone temples rise above the trees,

52
00:02:02,452 --> 00:02:05,236
painted red. Plazas fill with traders,

53
00:02:05,256 --> 00:02:06,859
priests and musicians.

54
00:02:07,278 --> 00:02:10,038
Laser scans from the air now suggest that,

55
00:02:10,058 --> 00:02:13,083
at their peak, the Maya lowlands may have held

56
00:02:13,103 --> 00:02:15,068
more than nine million people.

57
00:02:15,538 --> 00:02:17,300
But here's the honest part.

58
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:20,732
You probably aren't royal. Most people were farmers.

59
00:02:20,752 --> 00:02:22,579
And most likely, so are you.

60
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:26,725
Your city is also plugged into a huge trading

61
00:02:26,745 --> 00:02:29,106
world. Salt comes from the coast.

62
00:02:29,126 --> 00:02:32,785
Sharp black obsidian comes from volcanic highlands.

63
00:02:32,805 --> 00:02:36,103
Green jade comes from river valleys far to the

64
00:02:36,123 --> 00:02:36,536
south.

65
00:02:36,955 --> 00:02:39,304
Traders carry it all on their backs,

66
00:02:39,324 --> 00:02:41,147
because there are no horses,

67
00:02:41,167 --> 00:02:43,581
no oxen, and no carts for long trips.

68
00:02:45,801 --> 00:02:47,533
Your life runs on maize.

69
00:02:47,553 --> 00:02:49,868
In a famous Maya creation story,

70
00:02:49,888 --> 00:02:53,006
the gods make the first people out of corn.

71
00:02:53,425 --> 00:02:56,506
Maize is so central that the Maya maize god

72
00:02:56,526 --> 00:02:58,381
is often shown as a young,

73
00:02:58,401 --> 00:03:01,698
handsome man, with a long head shaped a little

74
00:03:01,718 --> 00:03:02,852
like a corn cob.

75
00:03:03,271 --> 00:03:05,253
Your family's field, the milpa,

76
00:03:05,273 --> 00:03:07,771
grows maize, beans and squash together.

77
00:03:08,192 --> 00:03:11,274
Every morning, the women of your house kneel at

78
00:03:11,294 --> 00:03:14,641
a grinding stone, and turn hard kernels into dough.

79
00:03:14,661 --> 00:03:16,819
It takes hours. Every single day.

80
00:03:17,240 --> 00:03:18,396
And you? As a kid,

81
00:03:18,416 --> 00:03:20,685
you're working too. Carrying water,

82
00:03:20,705 --> 00:03:22,581
chasing birds from the field,

83
00:03:22,601 --> 00:03:25,131
gathering firewood. Childhood is short.

84
00:03:25,551 --> 00:03:28,049
Tamales, maize porridge, a little chili.

85
00:03:28,069 --> 00:03:29,810
That's your menu, most days.

86
00:03:30,230 --> 00:03:32,710
And chocolate? It's not a candy bar.

87
00:03:32,730 --> 00:03:34,653
It's a bitter, frothy drink,

88
00:03:34,673 --> 00:03:37,013
often saved for nobles and feasts.

89
00:03:37,433 --> 00:03:39,524
But cacao beans weren't just food.

90
00:03:39,544 --> 00:03:42,379
Researchers think that, by the eighth century,

91
00:03:42,399 --> 00:03:44,427
cacao may have worked like money.

92
00:03:44,848 --> 00:03:48,289
Imagine paying for your lunch with chocolate beans.

93
00:03:48,309 --> 00:03:51,207
Now imagine trying not to eat your savings.

94
00:03:53,427 --> 00:03:56,460
Then there's the game. In the city's ballcourt,

95
00:03:56,480 --> 00:03:59,187
two teams fight over a heavy ball of solid

96
00:03:59,207 --> 00:03:59,641
rubber.

97
00:04:00,062 --> 00:04:03,179
No hands allowed. Players hit it mostly with their

98
00:04:03,199 --> 00:04:05,124
hips, wrapped in thick padding,

99
00:04:05,144 --> 00:04:06,379
and every hit hurts.

100
00:04:06,799 --> 00:04:08,678
Crowds cheer from the slopes.

101
00:04:08,698 --> 00:04:11,037
The rules changed from city to city,

102
00:04:11,057 --> 00:04:13,395
and we still don't know all of them.

103
00:04:13,816 --> 00:04:16,336
For you, it's sport and celebration.

104
00:04:16,356 --> 00:04:19,088
For the kings, it can also be politics,

105
00:04:19,108 --> 00:04:22,334
religion, and a replay of the gods' own myths.

106
00:04:24,553 --> 00:04:26,440
So what keeps you up at night?

107
00:04:26,460 --> 00:04:29,172
Jaguars in the forest. Snakes in the field.

108
00:04:29,192 --> 00:04:30,062
Fever. Hunger.

109
00:04:30,482 --> 00:04:33,205
And something bigger. In Maya stories,

110
00:04:33,225 --> 00:04:35,154
the dead travel to Xibalba,

111
00:04:35,174 --> 00:04:38,547
a dark underworld ruled by lords of disease and

112
00:04:38,567 --> 00:04:38,980
death.

113
00:04:39,401 --> 00:04:42,196
In one famous tale, two hero twins go down

114
00:04:42,216 --> 00:04:44,743
there, and beat the lords of death at,

115
00:04:44,763 --> 00:04:46,687
you guessed it, the ballgame.

116
00:04:48,908 --> 00:04:52,272
At night, look up. Maya astronomers watch the sky

117
00:04:52,292 --> 00:04:54,000
with incredible patience.

118
00:04:54,420 --> 00:04:57,352
They track Venus, eclipses and the seasons,

119
00:04:57,372 --> 00:05:00,031
and they run several calendars at once.

120
00:05:00,451 --> 00:05:03,624
They even write numbers with a symbol for zero,

121
00:05:03,644 --> 00:05:06,954
carved on a monument by around one hundred eighty

122
00:05:06,974 --> 00:05:09,468
A D, long before zero reached Europe.

123
00:05:09,939 --> 00:05:11,692
One of their great cycles,

124
00:05:11,712 --> 00:05:15,307
the Long Count, rolled over on December twenty first,

125
00:05:15,327 --> 00:05:18,036
twenty twelve. Not the end of the world.

126
00:05:18,056 --> 00:05:20,218
Just a new page in the calendar.

127
00:05:20,639 --> 00:05:24,165
Scribes paint glyphs in folding books made of bark

128
00:05:24,185 --> 00:05:27,569
paper. Books of history, astronomy and prophecy.

129
00:05:27,990 --> 00:05:30,010
Almost all of them are gone.

130
00:05:30,030 --> 00:05:33,654
In fifteen sixty two, a Spanish bishop burned Maya

131
00:05:33,674 --> 00:05:35,330
books in a public fire.

132
00:05:35,350 --> 00:05:38,027
Only four are known to survive today.

133
00:05:40,247 --> 00:05:42,416
Above everyone stands the king,

134
00:05:42,436 --> 00:05:45,806
the holy lord, dressed in jade and green quetzal

135
00:05:45,826 --> 00:05:46,442
feathers.

136
00:05:46,862 --> 00:05:49,889
Some nobles even had tiny discs of jade set

137
00:05:49,909 --> 00:05:52,724
into their front teeth. Imagine smiling,

138
00:05:52,744 --> 00:05:55,346
and flashing a little green treasure.

139
00:05:55,766 --> 00:05:58,883
He's seen as a bridge between people and the

140
00:05:58,903 --> 00:06:00,665
gods. To feed those gods,

141
00:06:00,685 --> 00:06:04,229
kings and queens offered their own blood in sacred

142
00:06:04,249 --> 00:06:04,800
rituals.

143
00:06:05,220 --> 00:06:08,410
And sometimes, captives from wars were sacrificed.

144
00:06:08,430 --> 00:06:10,336
It's a dark part of the story,

145
00:06:10,356 --> 00:06:13,162
but it's part of the world you're born into.

146
00:06:13,632 --> 00:06:15,006
For a farmer like you,

147
00:06:15,026 --> 00:06:17,034
the deal is simple. Pay tribute,

148
00:06:17,054 --> 00:06:19,696
help build, and trust the king to keep the

149
00:06:19,716 --> 00:06:20,520
rains coming.

150
00:06:22,740 --> 00:06:24,410
And then, the rains don't come.

151
00:06:25,029 --> 00:06:28,443
Between about eight hundred and nine hundred fifty A

152
00:06:28,463 --> 00:06:31,877
D, studies suggest rainfall in the region dropped by

153
00:06:31,897 --> 00:06:33,793
twenty five to forty percent.

154
00:06:34,213 --> 00:06:37,873
Crops fail. Reservoirs shrink. Cities fight each other.

155
00:06:37,893 --> 00:06:40,684
People stop believing the king can fix it.

156
00:06:41,103 --> 00:06:43,905
Archaeologists still debate the details.

157
00:06:43,925 --> 00:06:47,361
Drought, war, too many people on too little land.

158
00:06:47,381 --> 00:06:50,395
Most think it was several of these at once.

159
00:06:50,814 --> 00:06:54,206
Over a few generations, the great southern cities are

160
00:06:54,226 --> 00:06:57,811
abandoned. Maybe your own grandchildren pack up and walk

161
00:06:57,831 --> 00:06:58,133
away.

162
00:06:58,552 --> 00:07:01,074
The jungle grows back over the plazas,

163
00:07:01,094 --> 00:07:03,950
the temples, the ballcourts. For centuries,

164
00:07:03,970 --> 00:07:05,489
roots crack the stones.

165
00:07:06,408 --> 00:07:09,968
Some families move north, to cities like Chichen Itza,

166
00:07:09,988 --> 00:07:13,349
which grow powerful after the southern cities fall.

167
00:07:15,569 --> 00:07:17,902
But here's what many people get wrong.

168
00:07:17,922 --> 00:07:19,575
The Maya never disappeared.

169
00:07:20,045 --> 00:07:23,896
Today, more than eleven million Maya people live in

170
00:07:23,916 --> 00:07:26,705
Guatemala, Mexico, Belize and beyond,

171
00:07:26,725 --> 00:07:29,514
and many still speak Mayan languages.

172
00:07:29,935 --> 00:07:32,818
Tikal itself is now a World Heritage site,

173
00:07:32,838 --> 00:07:36,758
wrapped in rainforest, where howler monkeys roar from the

174
00:07:36,778 --> 00:07:38,141
tops of the temples.

175
00:07:38,610 --> 00:07:41,341
And the writing that bishop tried to burn?

176
00:07:41,361 --> 00:07:42,912
In the last few decades,

177
00:07:42,932 --> 00:07:46,383
scholars have learned to read most Maya glyphs again.

178
00:07:46,403 --> 00:07:48,806
The old kings are speaking once more.

179
00:07:49,276 --> 00:07:52,572
Some still plant milpa. Some still keep the sacred

180
00:07:52,592 --> 00:07:55,757
calendar. Some are probably watching this video.

181
00:07:56,226 --> 00:07:58,519
So if you'd been born in a Maya city

182
00:07:58,539 --> 00:08:01,666
thirteen hundred years ago, your city would fall.

183
00:08:01,686 --> 00:08:03,657
But your people, your language,

184
00:08:03,677 --> 00:08:05,841
and your corn would still be here.

185
00:08:06,461 --> 00:08:09,141
Which civilization should you be born in next?

186
00:08:09,161 --> 00:08:10,550
Tell me in the comments.
